ITEMS = [
{"id":"EMP-01","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - federal + state","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"time-sensitive numeric threshold (stale-figure trap)",
 "question":"We have a 'Marketing Manager' paid a $52,000 salary who supervises no one and spends most of her time executing campaigns. Is she exempt from overtime?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Exemption requires both a salary-basis/salary-level test and a duties test; job title is irrelevant","The federal salary level has changed repeatedly in recent years and is litigated - a responsible answer flags that the current figure must be checked against the DOL rule in force rather than asserting a number","Duties described (no supervision, executing rather than setting policy) point away from the executive exemption and make the administrative exemption doubtful, since it requires discretion and independent judgment on matters of significance","State law may impose a higher salary threshold - California and New York in particular - and the more protective standard governs","Misclassification exposure: unpaid overtime, liquidated damages, and a two- or three-year FLSA lookback"],
  "trap":"Asserting a specific current federal salary threshold as settled, or resolving exempt status from the title alone.",
  "authorities":["29 U.S.C. 213(a)(1)","29 C.F.R. Part 541","state wage orders"]}},
{"id":"EMP-02","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - California","difficulty":2,
 "probe":"jurisdiction-specific rule vs. general rule",
 "question":"Can we enforce a one-year, 25-mile non-compete against a California-based employee if our employment agreement is governed by Texas law?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["California voids non-competes regardless of reasonableness under Business and Professions Code 16600","Recent California legislation (SB 699 and AB 1076, effective 2024) extends this to contracts signed outside California and makes it unlawful to include or attempt to enforce a void non-compete","Employees have a private right of action including attorney's fees, and employers faced a notice obligation to affected current and former employees","Labor Code 925 restricts forcing a California employee into out-of-state law or forum for disputes arising in California","Trade secrets remain protectable, and a narrowly drawn customer non-solicit is still risky in California"],
  "trap":"Applying a reasonableness / blue-pencil analysis, or assuming the Texas choice-of-law clause resolves it.",
  "authorities":["Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code 16600, 16600.5","Cal. Lab. Code 925","Edwards v. Arthur Andersen, 44 Cal. 4th 937 (2008)"]}},
{"id":"EMP-03","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - multi-state","difficulty":2,
 "probe":"nationwide generalization that has a state exception",
 "question":"Is employment in the United States always at-will?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["At-will is the default in nearly every state but is not absolute","Montana is the recognized exception, with a statute requiring good cause after a probationary period","Common exceptions: public-policy tort, implied contract from handbooks or conduct, implied covenant of good faith in a minority of states","Statutory overlays always apply: anti-discrimination, retaliation, NLRA protected concerted activity, WARN","Union contracts and individual agreements can displace at-will"],
  "trap":"Stating flatly that all US employment is at-will with no exceptions.",
  "authorities":["Mont. Code Ann. 39-2-904 (Wrongful Discharge From Employment Act)"]}},
{"id":"EMP-04","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":2,
 "probe":"process obligation omitted",
 "question":"An employee has asked to work remotely three days a week as an accommodation. We think the role needs to be on-site. What is our obligation?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["The ADA requires an interactive process; refusing outright without engaging is itself a risk","Employer may request supporting documentation of the limitation and the need, within limits","Analysis turns on essential functions of the position, which should be documented and consistent with the actual job","Employer need not grant the employee's preferred accommodation if an effective alternative exists","Undue hardship is the employer's burden and is a high bar; generalized concerns are not enough","State law may be broader - California FEHA in particular"],
  "trap":"Answering that the employer can simply deny because attendance is an essential function, without the interactive process.",
  "authorities":["42 U.S.C. 12112(b)(5)","29 C.F.R. 1630.2(o)(3)"]}},
{"id":"EMP-05","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - federal + California","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"federal-only answer to a question with a stricter state overlay",
 "question":"We are closing a California site and laying off 62 people out of a 240-person company. Do we need to give notice?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Federal WARN: 100+ employees; triggers at a plant closing affecting 50+ at a single site, or a mass layoff of 500+, or 50-499 if that is at least 33% of the active workforce at the site; 60 days notice","On these facts federal WARN may be triggered by the plant-closing prong if it is a single-site closing affecting 50 or more","California WARN (Lab. Code 1400 et seq.) is broader: covers covered establishments with 75 or more employees in the preceding 12 months and is triggered at 50 affected employees in a 30-day period with no percentage test","California has no small-layoff exemption parallel to the federal 33% test, so state notice is very likely required here regardless","Notice content and recipients are prescribed: employees, EDD, local workforce board, chief elected official","Penalties: back pay and benefits for the violation period, plus civil penalties"],
  "trap":"Answering under federal WARN only and concluding no notice is required.",
  "authorities":["29 U.S.C. 2101-2109","Cal. Lab. Code 1400-1408"]}},
{"id":"EMP-06","practice_area":"Employment","jurisdiction":"US - California + federal","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"conflation of two different legal tests",
 "question":"We engage software developers in California as 1099 contractors. They use our tools, work our hours, and only work for us. Is that defensible?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["California applies the ABC test codified by AB 5 for most wage-order and Labor Code purposes","All three prongs must be met: (A) free from control, (B) work outside the usual course of the hiring entity's business, (C) customarily engaged in an independently established trade","On these facts prong B is the likely failure for a software company engaging developers, and prong A is also doubtful","Borello multifactor test still governs for some exempt categories and for certain statutes","Federal analysis is separate - the DOL economic-realities test and the IRS common-law control test - and a worker can be a contractor for one and an employee for another","Exposure: unpaid overtime, expense reimbursement under Labor Code 2802, payroll taxes, PAGA and class claims"],
  "trap":"Applying only the IRS control test, or blending the ABC and economic-realities tests as if they were one standard.",
  "authorities":["Cal. Lab. Code 2775 et seq. (AB 5)","Dynamex Operations West v. Superior Court, 4 Cal. 5th 903 (2018)","S.G. Borello & Sons v. DIR, 48 Cal. 3d 341 (1989)"]}},
{"id":"LIT-01","practice_area":"Litigation procedure","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":2,
 "probe":"pre-2015 standard recited as current",
 "question":"Opposing counsel says our document requests are overbroad. What is the current standard for the scope of discovery in federal court?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Rule 26(b)(1) as amended in 2015: nonprivileged matter relevant to any party's claim or defense AND proportional to the needs of the case","The six proportionality factors: importance of the issues, amount in controversy, parties' relative access to information, parties' resources, importance of the discovery in resolving the issues, and whether the burden outweighs the likely benefit","The phrase 'reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence' was deleted in 2015 and is no longer the test","Relevance to the 'subject matter' now requires good cause and a court order","Information need not be admissible to be discoverable"],
  "trap":"Quoting 'reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence' as the governing standard.",
  "authorities":["Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1)","2015 Advisory Committee Notes"]}},
{"id":"LIT-02","practice_area":"Litigation procedure","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":2,
 "probe":"procedural vehicle and timing",
 "question":"We already filed our answer but want to attack the complaint's sufficiency. What is our vehicle and what standard applies?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["A Rule 12(b)(6) motion must be made before the responsive pleading; after answering, the vehicle is Rule 12(c) judgment on the pleadings","Failure to state a claim is preserved and not waived under Rule 12(h)(2); it can also be raised at trial","The substantive standard on a 12(c) motion for failure to state a claim is the same as 12(b)(6)","Twombly/Iqbal plausibility: accept well-pleaded facts as true, disregard legal conclusions, ask whether the claim is plausible on its face","Materials outside the pleadings convert the motion to summary judgment under Rule 12(d), subject to narrow exceptions for incorporation by reference and judicial notice"],
  "trap":"Advising that a 12(b)(6) motion can still be filed after the answer, or that the argument is waived.",
  "authorities":["Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6), 12(c), 12(d), 12(h)(2)","Bell Atlantic v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544 (2007)","Ashcroft v. Iqbal, 556 U.S. 662 (2009)"]}},
{"id":"LIT-03","practice_area":"Litigation procedure","jurisdiction":"US - federal / constitutional","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"recent Supreme Court refinement",
 "question":"Our client is sued in Montana over a product it sold nationwide but not specifically into Montana; the particular unit was resold there by a third party. Can the court exercise specific personal jurisdiction?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Specific jurisdiction requires purposeful availment plus a claim that arises out of OR RELATES TO the defendant's forum contacts","Ford Motor Co. v. Montana Eighth Judicial District Court (2021) held that a strict causal link is not required where the defendant systematically served the market in the forum for the same product","Bristol-Myers Squibb remains the limit: nonresident plaintiffs with no forum connection cannot piggyback","Analysis is claim-by-claim and defendant-by-defendant, and must also satisfy fair play and substantial justice","General jurisdiction is a separate route, limited to the place of incorporation and principal place of business absent the exceptional case (Daimler)"],
  "trap":"Applying a but-for causation requirement and concluding there is no jurisdiction because the defendant did not sell that unit into the forum.",
  "authorities":["Ford Motor Co. v. Montana Eighth Judicial Dist. Ct., 592 U.S. 351 (2021)","Bristol-Myers Squibb v. Superior Court, 582 U.S. 255 (2017)","Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117 (2014)"]}},
{"id":"LIT-04","practice_area":"Litigation procedure","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"multiple interacting deadlines",
 "question":"We were served with a state-court complaint 40 days ago. Diversity is complete, the amount exceeds $75,000, and one co-defendant is a citizen of the forum state. Can we remove?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Removal notice is due within 30 days of service of the initial pleading under 28 U.S.C. 1446(b); at 40 days that window has run on the original pleading","The forum-defendant rule in 1441(b)(2) bars removal on diversity grounds where any properly joined and served defendant is a citizen of the forum state","All properly joined and served defendants must consent - the rule of unanimity","A separate 30-day window can open if an amended pleading, motion, order or other paper first makes the case removable","One-year outer limit on diversity removals under 1446(c)(1), subject to a bad-faith exception","Fraudulent joinder is the narrow argument if the forum defendant has no viable claim against it"],
  "trap":"Overlooking either the expired 30-day clock or the forum-defendant rule, and answering yes.",
  "authorities":["28 U.S.C. 1441(b)(2)","28 U.S.C. 1446(b)","28 U.S.C. 1446(c)(1)"]}},
{"id":"LIT-05","practice_area":"Litigation / e-discovery","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"sanctions standard stated without the intent element",
 "question":"Our client's auto-delete policy wiped a custodian's mailbox four months after the demand letter arrived. What sanctions are we exposed to?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Duty to preserve attaches when litigation is reasonably anticipated - the demand letter likely triggered it","Rule 37(e) is the exclusive framework for lost ESI and displaced prior inherent-authority spoliation standards","Threshold: ESI that should have been preserved was lost because reasonable steps were not taken and it cannot be restored or replaced through additional discovery","37(e)(1) - on a finding of prejudice, measures no greater than necessary to cure it","37(e)(2) - adverse-inference instruction, presumption, dismissal or default require a finding of INTENT to deprive; negligence or gross negligence is not enough","Practical response: suspend auto-delete, document the hold, investigate backups and alternative sources"],
  "trap":"Stating that an adverse-inference instruction is available on a showing of negligence.",
  "authorities":["Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e)","2015 Advisory Committee Notes to Rule 37(e)"]}},
{"id":"LIT-06","practice_area":"Privilege","jurisdiction":"US - federal","difficulty":3,
 "probe":"privilege overclaim",
 "question":"Our in-house counsel interviewed twelve employees about a compliance issue and sent a summary memo to the CFO. Is all of it privileged?",
 "gold":{"must_include":["Upjohn extends the corporate privilege to communications with employees at any level where the communication is made at the direction of counsel to obtain legal advice and concerns matters within the employee's duties","Underlying facts are never privileged; only the communications are","Upjohn warnings should have been given - the privilege belongs to the company, not the individual employees, and the company may waive","Business advice is not privileged; mixed memos require a primarily-legal-purpose analysis, and courts differ on 'primary purpose' vs 'a primary purpose'","Interview notes and the memo may separately be attorney work product under Rule 26(b)(3), with near-absolute protection for opinion work product","Disclosure to non-privileged recipients or use of the investigation as a defense can waive"],
  "trap":"Asserting blanket privilege over the entire investigation including the underlying facts.",
  "authorities":["Upjohn Co. v. United States, 449 U.S. 383 (1981)","Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3)","In re Kellogg Brown & Root, 756 F.3d 754 (D.C. Cir. 2014)"]}},
]
